    <statement><p>Find a parameterization <m>(x(t),y(t),z(t))</m> for the
curve
<m>y = 6-2x^{3}</m> that is parallel to
the <m>xy</m>-plane.  Your parametrized curve should pass through the point
<m>(0, 6 , -4)</m> when <m>t = -1</m>, and have <m>x'(-1)\neq 0</m>.</p>
<p><m>x(t) =</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0001"/>,</p>
<p><m>y(t) =</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0002"/>,</p>
<p><m>z(t) =</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>t--1</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>6+-2\mathopen{}\left(t--1\right)^{3}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-4</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>z</m> is constant since the curve is parallel to the <m>xy</m>-plane, so <m>z=-4</m> 
since it passes through <m>(0,6,-4)</m>. One possible answer
is
<me>x = t+1, \quad y = 6-2\mathopen{}\left(t+1\right)^{3}, \quad z=-4,</me>
which passes through the indicated point, with <m>x'(-1)=1</m>.



</solution>
